{
  "reasoning": "The lecture moves from continued measurement/evaluation of cybersickness to mitigation strategies and then to a closing summary. I kept the opening, recap, and outline in the first segment and made its substantive sub-goal the comparison of ML-based evaluation models (interpretable, black-box, and hybrid), because the lecturer presents them as one continuing discussion of evaluation models. Clear section bridges occur at sentence 55 (classification by event), 72 (evaluation output), 86 (mitigation), 99 (developer perspective), 179 (user perspective), and 218 (final summary/preview). The individual mitigation techniques are treated as illustrative details of the developer- or user-perspective sub-goals rather than separate segments, since the course step is to understand mitigation from those perspectives.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 1,
      "subgoal": "Compare interpretable, black-box, and hybrid ML models used to measure cybersickness."
    },
    {
      "start": 55,
      "subgoal": "Distinguish detection from prediction of cybersickness and explain why prediction matters."
    },
    {
      "start": 72,
      "subgoal": "Describe the possible outputs of cybersickness evaluation, including ratings, classes, and sickness scores."
    },
    {
      "start": 86,
      "subgoal": "Explain the challenge of mitigating cybersickness, including the presence trade-off and developer/user perspectives."
    },
    {
      "start": 99,
      "subgoal": "Describe developer-side strategies for reducing cybersickness, such as FOV adjustment, visual-cue changes, hardware alterations, and locomotion modifications."
    },
    {
      "start": 179,
      "subgoal": "Describe user-side measures for reducing cybersickness, including exposure limits, habituation, breathing, and environmental controls."
    },
    {
      "start": 218,
      "subgoal": "Summarize the main points of the lecture and preview the next topic."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "reasoning": "I merged the previously separate output discussion, sentences 72-85, into the preceding evaluation segment, sentences 55-85, because the output part was short and, together with the detection-versus-prediction distinction, completes the same larger step of finishing the cybersickness evaluation discussion. The combined segment still starts at a clear bridge and ends where the lecturer explicitly closes the evaluation topic. I kept segment 1 as the opening plus the comparison of ML-based evaluation models, kept the mitigation introduction as a separate sub-goal because it sets up the trade-off and the two perspectives, kept the developer and user mitigation segments as separate closed sub-goals, and kept the final summary as the closing segment.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 1,
      "subgoal": "Explain how ML-based cybersickness evaluation compares interpretable, black-box, and hybrid models."
    },
    {
      "start": 55,
      "subgoal": "Explain how cybersickness evaluation is categorized by detection versus prediction and by possible outputs such as classes or sickness scores."
